# Project - flink-application

A Flink application project using Java and Gradle, for Flink **2.1.0** (same version as the
`jobmanager` / `taskmanager` images in `../docker-compose.yml`).

## Status

Last checked: 2026-11-08, built with JDK 17 and run on the local Flink 2.1.0 cluster.

| Item                                          | Status                                                         |
|-----------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|
| Build (`./gradlew shadowJar`)                 | OK                                                             |
| `com.github.frtu.vm.sample.DataStreamJob`     | **OK**: main class, word count on built-in sample lines, job `FINISHED` |
| `jobs.WordCountJob`                           | Compiles; same code as `DataStreamJob`                         |
| `WordCount` (default package)                 | Compiles; reads a socket on `127.0.0.1:9000`, so it can't run on the cluster as it is |
| `jobs.CustomerDebeziumJob`                    | Not implemented: all code commented out (Kafka + `debezium-json` table) |
| `jobs.CustomerAnalyticsJob`                   | Not implemented: all code commented out (`datagen` table)      |

Known gaps:

* The shadow jar is named `build/libs/flink-application-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT-all.jar`, but
  `scripts/flink.sh submit` looks for `build/libs/flink-application.jar`. Copy it to that
  name before submitting (see below).
* `flink.sh submit` runs the jar's `Main-Class` (`DataStreamJob`) and can't pick another class.
* No Kafka connector dependency yet (`flinkShadowJar "org.apache.flink:flink-connector-kafka..."`
  is commented out in `build.gradle`), so the CDC jobs can't be enabled as they are.

## Test end to end

Run from the stack directory (`docker/data-pipeline`).

1. Have a JDK 17+ ready (the host has no default Java; SDKMAN provides one):

   ```bash
   export JAVA_HOME=~/.sdkman/candidates/java/17.0.18-zulu
   export PATH=$JAVA_HOME/bin:$PATH
   ```

2. Start the Flink cluster and wait until it's healthy (`flink  OK`):

   ```bash
   scripts/stack.sh up jobmanager taskmanager
   scripts/health.sh --wait
   ```

   `health.sh` also reports the other components. Those can show FAIL if you started only Flink.

3. Build the jar and copy it to the name `flink.sh submit` looks for:

   ```bash
   scripts/flink.sh build flink-application
   cp flink-application/build/libs/flink-application-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT-all.jar \
      flink-application/build/libs/flink-application.jar
   ```

4. Submit the job (the stack directory is mounted at `/workspace` in the jobmanager):

   ```bash
   scripts/flink.sh submit flink-application
   # -> Job has been submitted with JobID <id>
   ```

5. Check the result:

   ```bash
   scripts/flink.sh jobs
   # <id>  FINISHED  Flink 2.0 WordCount with Static Data
   scripts/stack.sh logs taskmanager 400 | grep -E '\(flink,|\(stream,'
   # taskmanager-2  | (flink,3)
   # taskmanager-2  | (stream,3)
   ```

   The job is also visible in the Flink UI: http://localhost:28081

## Local run

To run with an embedded Flink instance (no cluster): `./gradlew run`.
